
FIXED (2026-10-22): Surface atmospheres now condense gases above their saturation partial pressures and iterate pressure, composition, and greenhouse temperature to convergence. Negligible residual atmospheres become vacuum, including after cosmic-shoreline estimates. Stored surface temperatures also enforce phase limits; incomplete abundance lists cannot leave an anonymous noncondensing atmosphere. Warm supplied temperatures are preserved.

Original request: If surface temperature is too cold for any of the atmospheric gases that are present to stay gaseous, then those gases should be limited to at most trace amounts in the atmosphere, and in most cases to zero. If the surface temperature is too cold for any gases, then the atmospheric pressure should be zero, irrespective of cosmic shoreline. This reduction of cold atmospheres will further lower the surface pressure, so it will be important to find the values where the feedback loop converges.

FIXED (2026-10-22): New stellar planets and the HZ button use the conservative habitable zone. The orbit editor warns about close or crossing planetary orbits, and the resonance picker filters choices using the same spacing check.

Original request: From the Shift+A dialog, adding a new body around a planet defaults to a 7 day orbit and tidal locking. That's good, keep that. If adding a planet around a star, let's default it to the habitable zone. We can also show a visible warning when a planet being edited is too close to another planet's orbit, resulting in orbital instability. Make sure the ratios in the resonance picker would not be close enough to existing bodies to require this same warning.

FIXED (2026-10-22): Terrain generation, water-cloud checks, horizon temperatures, and sun-clock tooltips share a bounded local climate model with pressure-dependent heat transport, elevation cooling in meters, and warm subtropical land. Frost blends gradually across irregular spherical climate boundaries. Earth bump-map and generated-ocean checks pass.

Original request: The rocky map generator depends on a local temperature estimate to decide kinds of terrain (desert, tundra, forest, etc). There is a similar local temperature estimate for mouseover in sun clock view and current location in horizon view, however that calculation does not produce good results. Let's fix it, and if possible without degrading the map generator's performance, get the map generator using the upgraded local temperature estimate. You can use Earth and its bump map as a benchmark: desert areas will be hot, polar ice caps will be cold, etc. One thing I want to avoid is sharp straight lines between ice/water and rock in generated maps. 

FIXED (2026-10-22): WD and Sunlike filters are mutually exclusive; white-dwarf rows display stellar color swatches. Empty filtered lists disable object actions.

Original request: Add WD (for white dwarf) as an option in Stellar Neighborhood, mutually exclusive with Sunlike: selecting one deselects the other. Show each white dwarf's color in its list entry if possible.

FIXED (2026-10-22): Explicit mass and radius edits now update their measurement status and clear obsolete uncertainties. A supplied dense radius overrides the estimated-envelope prior, so the six-Earth-mass case remains rocky. The fault was stale estimated-radius metadata, not a valid high-density ice-giant classification.

Original request: Double check Planet::classify(). A planet of 6 Earth masses becomes an ice giant even if the density runs in the thousands of g/cm^3. If this is correct, explain why here.

FIXED (2026-10-22): Changing the Add Object type no longer enables star labels. The star-label picker retains its intentional enable-on-selection behavior.

Original request: Clicking certain lists and dropdowns reverts star labels back on. Only the star label list should do this.


FIXED (2026-10-22): Grayscale PNG bump maps now apply the sea-level offset before scaling to meters, removing a spurious elevation increase of about 10 km.


FIXED (2026-10-22): Extreme zoom uses consistent projection for stellar discs and flare centers, preserves tiny angular separations, scales the culling margin in pixels, and rejects offscreen disc and glare work. Regression checks cover magnification 2.5 million and distant background stars.

Original request: Zooming in on a star causes performance degradation and incorrect flare placement. Vega at magnification 2.5 million is a good example. Frame rate temporarily crawls to almost nothing (in some cases this can hang the app) and the flare is centered below and to the right of the star's disc.


FIXED (2026-10-22): Earth-view photometry regression scans 142,963 catalog stars and 4,380 galaxies at three epochs, including 3,042 variable stars. It allows known variability extrema and explicit magnitude tolerances for estimated V and integrated B photometry. All records pass.

Original request: Add a regression test that no stellar object or galactic object displays, from Earth, with a significantly brighter magnitude than its catalog vmag, allowing for variability and color band differences.

